{"text": "A strong profile begins with what the student can do and what helps the student engage. The profile lists these strengths for Olivia: Strong oral vocabulary and memory; Enjoys astronomy and collaborative science; Persistent problem-solver; and Values choice and independence. They give the team meaningful entry points for instruction, motivation, peer connection, and self-advocacy.\n\nHelpful preferences are: Work positioned at eye level; Predictable personal-care schedule; Digital materials; and Peers who ask before helping. Respecting these preferences can increase participation and support student choice.\n\nCommunication access must be planned explicitly. Olivia’s communication profile includes the following: Speaks clearly; voice becomes quieter with fatigue; Uses speech-to-text for longer responses; Signals discomfort with a private agreed phrase; and Needs wait time rather than others finishing sentences. Staff should communicate directly with Olivia, allow the required processing or response time, and confirm meaning without speaking for the student. If communication becomes less effective, check access, fatigue, stress, sensory load, and the communication environment.\n\nThe key principle is to presume competence while making communication reliable. Next, I’ll explain the conditions in which Olivia learns best and the barriers that can make learning harder.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-A-03.mp3", "words": [{"text": "A", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.225, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 1}, {"text": "strong", "start": 0.2375, "end": 0.6, "start_char": 2, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "profile", "start": 0.6125, "end": 1.225, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 16}, {"text": "begins", "start": 1.2375, "end": 1.6875, "start_char": 17, "end_char": 23}, {"text": "with", "start": 1.7, "end": 1.85, "start_char": 24, "end_char": 28}, {"text": "what", "start": 1.8625, "end": 2.0625, "start_char": 29, "end_char": 33}, {"text": "the", "start": 2.0